#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139702 :tutorial/content "To evaluate a power, we write it out with multiplication signs and then compute the resulting product.\n\nSo, to find the value of $9^2,$ we first write it out with multiplication signs as follows:\n![](images/c7/c7e4007e9b80b07aca028ffc9bf8f5c4354bafb4e15dd0ce7e474279e364e1a3.svg)\n\nThen, we compute the product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{2}=9\\times 9=81\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRaising a number to the power of $2$ is known as **squaring** a number. Therefore, the following are equivalent.\n\n- $9^2 = 81$\n\n- The **square** of $9$ is $81.$\n\n- $9$ raised to the *second* power equals $81.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c1b4655c-0ae1-50ce-a37f-b3d77fdc30d7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8587 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139743 :tutorial/content "Let's evaluate the following exponent:\n\n$$\n2^{\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nWe start by writing our exponent as repeated multiplication:\n![](images/fe/fea572ac021fd3eabd4d5b1ade6692cbb1507d993b16616f63b8aa78259d3ade.svg)\n\nNow, we compute the product. \n\nSince $2 \\times 2 = 4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{2\\times 2}_{⏟}}_{4}\\times 2 & =4\\times 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${\\color{blue}{4}}\\times 2=8,$ we have $2^3=8.$\n\nRaising a number to the power of $3$ is known as **cubing** a number. Therefore, the following are equivalent.\n\n- $2^3 = 8$\n\n- The **cube** of $2$ is $8.$\n\n- $2$ raised to the *third* power equals $8.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2cf5f36c-da57-5e4b-91e8-76c86e67837f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9457 :tutorial/title "The Cube of a Number"}]}
